00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:21.070
schema.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.620
I will also highlight the
tools available in Spanner

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.840
for efficient schema
design and how

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.810
to keep the schema updated
with online schema changes.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.490
To start, Cloud Spanner is a
role-based relational database.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.830
Spanner requires
a defined schema

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.990
and supports
standard SQL concepts

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.290
for defining that schema.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.350
Table columns must have
a data type defined.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.100
Spanner supports standard
scalar data types, as well as

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.950
more advanced data types to
enable different application

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.150
requirements.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.720
For instance, JSON allows
storing schema-less data

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.750
within Spanner.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.690
Constraints, foreign
keys, and unique indexes

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.210
help enforce the
integrity of the data,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.970
and unique and nonunique
indexes help provide

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.841
faster lookups of the data.

00:01:11.841 --> 00:01:15.190
A key reason to use Spanner
is the near unlimited scaling

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.920
capability for
application workloads.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.830
When designing that schema,
it's important to understand

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:24.950
how Spanner organizes data
to achieve the scalability.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.810
As a horizontally
scaling database,

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.020
Spanner automatically
shards data into splits.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.260
Splits enable Spanner
to scale by distributing

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.510
compute capacity to
multiple servers.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.150
But how do these splits
affect the design decisions

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.380
for my schema?

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.240
For a quick example
from my banking app,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.800
I have a Customers table with
a CustomerId as primary key.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.650
A common pattern in many
relational databases

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.690
is to increment an
integer for new rows.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.180
With data splits,
sequential keys

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.490
are an antipattern
because your last split

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:03.760
will receive all new
rows, creating a hot spot.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.730
Ideally, the primary key should
be inserted in random order

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:13.000
so inserts hit multiple splits,
which improves right-scaling.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.310
Common methods to get
random order inserts

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:20.770
are UUID version 4 or
generating hash keys.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.530
Splits also have
implications for related data

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.000
in other tables.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.900
In my app, a customer can
have multiple accounts.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.720
The accounts data could
end up in a different split

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.260
than the customer it belongs to.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.140
If I have a query to get a
customer and their accounts,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.350
Spanner will have to get
data from multiple splits.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.920
This increases request latency,
especially if the splits are

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.050
processed by different servers.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.020
To avoid this, tables can
be interleaved together

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.510
in the schema definition.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.990
If I interleave Accounts
into the Customers table,

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:59.840
then the account rows will
be located in the same split

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.620
as the customer they belong
to and make my query faster.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.950
Keep in mind that interleaving
cannot be changed for existing

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.520
tables and not all table
relationships should be

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:11.780
interleaved.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.500
Interleaving should be used for
related data that is frequently

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.150
accessed together.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.390
Splits also affect indexes
because Spanner implements

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:25.740
indexes as tables internally,
so requesting more columns

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.350
than an index provides
can result in getting data

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.540
from multiple splits.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.730
Creating an index
that stores columns

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.210
that are typically requested
by queries can solve this.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.790
In my app, the
UniqueUsername index

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.420
also stores the
password so a login

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.000
query that searches for
a combination of username

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.670
and password will
be done efficiently.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.430
The last thing to highlight
about Spanner schema design

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.710
is that schema
changes are online,

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.770
so your application can
continue reading and writing

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.100
to the table during the change.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.230
The time to complete a change
depends on the type of change.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.140
Adding a new column that
doesn't have a default value

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.180
will complete quickly
since there's nothing

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.520
to do for existing rows.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.760
Adding a new column or
modifying an existing column

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.790
with a default value
requires backfilling data

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.920
into each existing
row of the table.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.680
Modifying an existing column
with a new constraint,

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.700
such as NOT NULL, will
require data validation.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.160
Data validation and
backfilling operations

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.530
take longer to
complete, but Spanner

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.390
will process this
in the background

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.720
so that your users and
applications are not impacted.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.820
This makes schema
changes painless.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.650
As a relational
database, Spanner

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:45.100
requires a defined schema
using supported data types,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.140
constraints, and indexing.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.960
But it makes it as easy as
possible to change later

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.420
as data requirements change.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.700
Data in Spanner is
automatically sharded

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.850
into splits that can be powered
by different compute nodes

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.980
to achieve great scalability.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.650
Splits require different
schema design choices

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.410
than traditional relational
database systems,

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.720
and I've shown how
to avoid hot-spotting

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.280
and how to interleave
related data together

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.520
so query requests are
handled efficiently.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.760
In the final video, I will
show how our banking app uses

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.890
both standard DML statements
and Spanner mutations to write

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:20.890
to our Spanner database.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:24.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

